  "subtitle": "The arcade cabinet that barrel-rolled with you inside",
  "why_scout_cares": "The theoretical extreme of whole-body kinesthetic HCI. A 1,100 kg gyroscopic sphere that rotated freely through 360 degrees on two axes, physically orienting the player's body to match in-game aircraft attitude. Strapped into a four-point harness inside a fiberglass cockpit, you didn't just play the game — the game moved you. Only ~100 units were built. It was so dangerous that Sega required a safety fence, two emergency stop buttons, a light-sensor grid, and a dedicated attendant at every installation. Warning labels barred players with heart conditions, pregnancy, high blood pressure, or intoxication. This is what happens when interface ambition collides with the biological limits of the human body.",
